'''vacillatory''' (vas′i-lā̤-tọ̄-ri), ''a.'' [< ''vacillate'' + ''-ory''.] Inclined to vacillate; wavering; vacillating; uncertain; irresolute. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Such ''vacillatory'' accounts of affairs of state.
{{right|''Roger North'', Examen, p. 25.}}}}

'''vacoa''' (vak′ọ̄-ä̤), ''n.'' [Native name.] A general name in Mauritius for the screw-pines (''Pandanus''), which there abound in numerous species, forming trees 20 or 30 feet high or more. {{smaller|''P. utilis'', introduced from Madagascar, growing, if permitted, 30 feet or more high, is commonly planted for its leaves, which are fabricated into sugar-sacks or vacoa bags. See cut under ''Pandanus.''}}

'''vacua''', ''n.'' An occasional plural of ''vacuum.''

'''vacuate''' (vak′ū-āt), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''vacuated'', ppr. ''vacuating.'' [< L. ''vacuatus'', pp. of ''vacuare'', make empty or void, < ''vacuus'', empty: see ''vacuous.''] To make empty or void; evacuate. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Mistaken zeal, . . . like the Pharisee’s Corban, under the pretense of an extraordinary service to God, ''vacuates'' all duty to man.
{{right|''Secular Priest Exposed'' (1703), p. 27. (''Latham.'')}}}}

